        "raw_text_sha256": "cd296fb56c77eeba33ebac0c8b2c0d2274e04671504bd808e1767afc46a93f6a",
        "current_text_sha256": "a8c1fb5a7df3a8c285b5e55e1487fa585e0c892ff572fcdf39af1d3687304f68"
      },
      "raw_text": "994. 使下列各式成立的$x$值存在吗？а) $\\sqrt{(x-5)^2}-x=25$； б) $x-\\sqrt{(x-1)^2}=9$。",
      "text": "994. 使下列各式成立的$x$值存在吗？\na) $\\sqrt{(x-5)^2}-x=25$；\nb) $x-\\sqrt{(x-1)^2}=9$。",
      "pages": ["p0299#3"],
      "figure_refs": [],
      "parts": [
        {
          "part": "a",
          "source_label": "а",
          "answer": "Yes. The unique real solution is $x=-10$.",
          "solution_set": "$\\{-10\\}$",
          "derivation": [
            "For every real $x$, the radicand $(x-5)^2\\geq0$ and its principal square root is $|x-5|$.",
            "For $x\\geq5$, the left side is $(x-5)-x=-5$, which cannot equal 25.",
            "For $x<5$, the left side is $(5-x)-x=5-2x$. Setting this equal to 25 gives $x=-10$, and $-10<5$ satisfies the branch condition.",
            "Substituting into the original expression gives $\\sqrt{(-10-5)^2}-(-10)=\\sqrt{225}+10=15+10=25$. The two branches exhaust all real numbers, so this solution exists and is unique."
          ],
          "domain": {
            "natural": "$x\\in\\mathbb R$; the square radicand imposes no restriction.",
            "stipulated": "No additional constraint on $x$.",
            "denominators": "None.",
            "principal_root_signs": "$\\sqrt{(x-5)^2}=|x-5|\\geq0$; the sign-changing inner quantity is not substituted for its absolute value.",
            "left_side_range": "$[-5,\\infty)$."
          },
          "equality_cases": "The equality to 25 holds exactly at $x=-10$.",
          "uncertainty": []
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "source_label": "б",
          "answer": "No real solution exists.",
          "solution_set": "$\\varnothing$",
          "derivation": [
            "For every real $x$, $\\sqrt{(x-1)^2}=|x-1|$.",
            "For $x\\geq1$, the left side is $x-(x-1)=1$, so it cannot equal 9.",
            "For $x<1$, the left side is $x-(1-x)=2x-1<1$. Solving $2x-1=9$ formally yields $x=5$, which violates this branch's condition and is therefore not a solution.",
            "Both branches show the entire left-side range is $(-\\infty,1]$. Since $9>1$, no allowed real $x$ satisfies the original equality."
          ],
          "domain": {
            "natural": "$x\\in\\mathbb R$; the square radicand is always nonnegative.",
            "stipulated": "No additional constraint on $x$.",
            "denominators": "None.",
            "principal_root_signs": "The principal root is $|x-1|\\geq0$; both branches, including $x=1$, are retained.",
            "left_side_range": "$(-\\infty,1]$."
          },
          "equality_cases": "Equality to 9 never occurs. The left side equals its maximum 1 exactly for $x\\geq1$.",
          "uncertainty": []
        }
      ],
      "uncertainty": []
    }
